# Peasant thrive: an agent-played game of Dynasty. Scenario and steering: scenario.md; deck: seed.md.
# Every say and aside is the reply of the agent playing that seat (see log/yearN/).
# The Storyteller writes the act lines and refs the moves; the engine keeps the books.
# The narrator's lines are not part of the game: they live in narration.md, never here.
# Each chapter opens with a "# ===== CHAPTER N: <title>" marker.
setup([1, 5, 6, 7], "peasant-thrive", "The peasant game",
      "Four players · nine years · survival",
      "The Emperor and the three peasants again, no Warlord, on a deck where the population doubles before it grows by half, so new land opens while the harvests still feed everyone. "
      "Reina builds a work every year; four seats change hands, and the Rice Peasant of Year One, Paddy, takes the throne in Year 6. "
      "He raises the whole Great Wall in Year 8, and in Year 9 the Marble Pagoda, the last of the 520 owed, ends the game before Reina can buy back the throne.",
      short="4 players")

# ============================== SETUP ==============================
R(0, "Setup", None, "Four players: the Emperor and the three peasants (rulebook Section 4 and Appendix A). No Warlord, so nobody raids and no soldiers are sold. No Official, Gentry or Scholar, so nobody collects rent, and the Emperor appoints a banker for the Harvest Pile, a tax collector and someone to read I Ching and count the population, at the Scholar's salary of 5 (Note 3). Nothing is owed to Provinces 2, 3 or 4. Each realia province holds one piece of its own realia and two of the one type it never needs. With no upper class at the table, the Silk Peasant stands in for it: a Silk Peasant who completes the objective may take the throne (Section 11).")
act("Four booklets: Reina Osorio draws the Emperor, Silke Weber the Silk Peasant, Paddy Doyle the Rice Peasant and Graham Garner the Wheat Peasant.", map_hl=[1,5,6,7])
deal_grain("Reina sets out the Harvest Pile and deals 60 to each player. It is the first year's harvest.")
take_stock("Silke, Paddy and Graham take their realia out of the box: one piece of their own and two of the type they'll never need. It waits beside the board until it's sold.")
act("Graham cuts out the Province 7 Reservoir, two squares by two, and sets it aside until it's built.", map_hl=[7])
act("Reina hands out the table jobs of the empty seats for the whole game: Graham Garner banks the Harvest Pile and pays out every harvest, Silke Weber collects the tax, and Paddy Doyle reads the I Ching card each year and keeps the population counter, for the Scholar's 5 a year from the crown.", map_hl=[1,5,6,7])
say("Reina", "Right, one job each, so nobody can say the crown has favourites. Graham, you hold the Harvest Pile and pay it out fairly. Silke, you bring the tax in honestly. Paddy, you read the I Ching, and the crown pays you five a year so the cards stay kind to all of us. Ten years is a long time, friends, and I mean to see the Wall finished with every one of you still at the table.",
 "Reina: Giving each peasant a job means each has a stake in the order holding, and the five a year to Paddy is cheap insurance on the man who keeps the population counter. Five out of sixty hurts a little now, but a revolt costs far more than that.")
act("Paddy shuffles the I Ching deck and sets it face down. The first card is turned in Year 2.", map_hl=[6])

# ===== CHAPTER 1: Three Deals Honoured at One Table
# ============================== YEAR 1 ==============================
R(1, "The first year", None, "No card is drawn. The 60 each player was dealt is the first harvest, and the tax is levied on it.")
CUR_INC.update({s:60 for s in ACTIVE})
say("Reina", "Thirty percent, friends: fair on a good year and nothing that pinches. The first thing the crown builds is the Reservoir in Province 7, because a work that feeds people pays for itself. Graham, enjoy the water. Palaces can wait a year, and so can my vanity.",
 "Reina: Fifty-four comes in, sixty goes on the Reservoir and five to Paddy, which leaves me forty-nine. The extra twenty a year in Graham's harvest is taxable for the rest of the game, so the crown earns it back. Next year the Winter Palace, to open the canals for Silke and Paddy.")
taxes(.30, collector="Silke collects the tax for the crown")
build("Province 7 Reservoir", 60, {1:60}, key="res7", at="O16:P17", text="Public works: Reina builds the Province 7 Reservoir, two squares by two, on O16 to P17, and the crown pays the whole 60. It adds 20 to Graham's harvest from next year.", hover="The 60 goes into the Harvest Pile. 460 of the works are still owed.")
say("Silke", "A Customs House, friends, and the coast keeps its books honest. Four to open. Cheap, for the piece that tells you what everyone else is moving.",
 "Silke: I've got two of these and one Jar, so the Customs House goes first and I keep the rare piece back until the table's hungrier for it. I'm keeping the opening low because what I want is grain in my hand, not a lot that sits unsold and has to go up again next year at two.")
say("Paddy", "Thank you, Reina, I'll read the cards straight, whatever they say. I'm putting up a Bamboo Brush, starting at four, same as Silke. I've two of them, so whoever misses this one gets another go next year.",
 "Paddy: I'm selling a brush first because I have two of them to get rid of, and I'm keeping the single Wooden Bowl back in case somebody turns out to really want it. I'm watching Silke. She opened at four, and I'd like to see what she pays for things before I spend a grain of my own.")
say("Graham", "Thank you for the water, Reina. I'll keep the Harvest Pile to the last grain, and you can count it after me any time you like. I'm putting up a Land Deed Scroll at four, same as my neighbours. Land is what this game ends up being about, so it seems fair to sell the paper for it early.",
 "Graham: I've got two Scrolls and only one Vase, so the Scroll goes first, the way Silke and Paddy each led with the piece they have two of. Every one of us has a piece the others need, so I'll watch what they pay for theirs before I decide what mine is worth.")
act("The auctions. Each seller holds two types beside the board and chooses which goes up: Silke a Customs House, Paddy a Bamboo Brush and Graham a Land Deed Scroll, each with a minimum of 4. Silke keeps the Dragon Jar back, Paddy the Wooden Bowl and Graham the Vase. The Emperor doesn't bid.", map_hl=[5,6,7])
lot(5, "CH", [
 B(7, 7, "Each of these pieces has another copy behind it, friends, so I won't bleed the table for any of them. Seven's my limit, no further. Paddy, if you'd rather not race me on the Customs House, take this one cheap and leave me first go at next year's. I'll say the same to Silke about the Brush.",
   "Graham: Three quarters of every grain bid goes into the pile and doesn't come back, so a war over a piece with two more copies behind it just makes us all poorer. I'll pay a fair price, and if someone outbids me I'll wait for the next copy."),
 B(6, 8, "There are two of everything here, friends, so there's no need to bleed each other dry. Here's an offer for the whole table: Graham, if you take this Customs House cheap, I'll stay out of it and have the second one next year. Silke, if you let me have this Land Deed, I'll stay out when Graham's second one comes up. If you'd rather bid, that's fine too, but I won't go far.",
   "Paddy: Every grain we throw at each other goes to the Harvest Pile, so I'm offering to split the two pairs fairly instead of fighting. I'm keeping my ceilings low because I still have to buy the Jar and the Vase and keep twenty grain back, and I'd rather wait a year for a piece than overpay for it."),
 B(7, None, "Fine, it's yours. I'll be waiting for the next one.")],
 hover=TH(("", "Graham's limit was 7, Paddy's 8."),
          ("Paddy", "It goes on Q19.")), at="Q19")
lot(6, "BB", [
 B(7, 7),
 B(5, 8, "There are two of each, friends, so let's not bleed the Harvest Pile over the first one. Graham, if you want this Brush badly, take it and I'll have the second next year. Paddy, the same goes for the Deed. If not, I'll go to eight and no further.",
   "Silke: I need both of these, but each one has a twin coming, so a war over the first copy is grain thrown into the Pile. I've capped myself at eight so I keep a cushion for tax and for land once the population doubles."),
 B(7, None)],
 hover=TH(("", "Graham's limit was 7, Silke's 8."),
          ("Silke", "It goes on P26, in the corner of the coast.")), at="P26")
lot(7, "LD", [
 B(5, 8), B(6, 9),
 B(5, None, "Fine, it's yours. The second one has my name on it.")],
 hover=TH(("", "Silke's limit was 8; Paddy would have gone to 10."),
          ("Paddy", "It goes on Q20, beside the Customs House.")), at="Q20")
say("Paddy", "Well, I didn't expect to win both of those, so thank you, both of you, and sorry it cost more than any of us wanted. Next year's Customs House is Graham's and next year's Deed is Silke's. I've got mine now, so I'll stay out of both, as I said I would.",
 "Paddy: That's two of my four pieces in the first year, which I didn't expect, but it cost me seventeen and I've only got twenty-seven grain left. Now I have to be careful with every grain until the Dragon Jar and the Vase come up.")
say("Silke", "The Brush goes down on the coast, tucked in the corner where nobody's going to build over it. Paddy, you'll have the Deed, and I'm holding you to it: Graham's second one is mine.",
 "Silke: I've put it in the far corner so it stays out of the way of any public works and leaves my open squares free for land later. Paddy beat me to the Deed by one grain, so I'll be watching that second copy closely.")
salaries({6:5}, text="Reina pays Paddy 5 for reading the I Ching, the Scholar's salary.", hover="Nobody else at this table is on a salary.")

# ============================== YEAR 2 ==============================
R(2, "Storms and raiders", "repairs")
act("Paddy turns the first I Ching card: Storms and Raiders. Every province collects a good harvest. Silke, Paddy and Graham each pay 2 to the Harvest Pile to repair their public works (Province 4 is empty); Reina plays the first personal disaster in the Emperor's booklet (Provinces 2, 3 and 8 are empty). The population holds at 100 million, x1.", map_hl=[6])
say("Paddy", "Storms and Raiders, friends. The good news first: every province gets a good harvest this year. Provinces 4, 5, 6 and 7 each pay two to repair their public works, and that includes me. Provinces 1, 2, 3 and 8 play a Personal Disaster, so Reina, I'm sorry, that's you, and you'll need to turn the next one in your booklet. Population stays at a hundred million, times one, so everybody reads the first row of the income table.",
 "Paddy: A good harvest is welcome when I'm down to thirty-two grain, and paying two for repairs won't hurt much. I'm a bit worried about Reina's disaster, though, because if the crown has to tighten its belt, the tax goes up for all of us.")
harvest("Graham pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile.")
act("Storms and Raiders: Silke, Paddy and Graham each pay 2 to the Harvest Pile for repairs.", deltas={pid(5):-2, pid(6):-2, pid(7):-2}, pile_delta=6, map_hl=[5,6,7])
disaster(1, "a rare horse, sent up the Silk Road as tribute, throws the Emperor in her excitement to ride it.")
say("Reina", "Thirty again. Steady is how I mean to run this house. This year it's the Winter Palace, and before you call it vanity, it's the gate to the canals. Silke, Paddy, your water comes next, one section at a time.",
 "Reina: Sixty in, twenty on the Palace, five to Paddy, which puts the reserve near a hundred and twenty after that horse cost me four. That's my cushion for doubled population and new land. With the Winter Palace up, I can put canals into Provinces 5 and 6 every year, and each one raises a taxable harvest.")
taxes(.30, collector="Silke collects the tax for the crown")
build("Winter Palace", 20, {1:20}, key="Winter Palace", at="H20", text="Public works: Reina builds the Winter Palace on H20, in Province 1, and the crown pays the 20. It must stand before the canals in Provinces 5 and 6.", hover="The 20 goes into the Harvest Pile. 440 of the works are still owed.")
act("The auctions. Silke puts up her second Customs House, minimum 4, and keeps the Dragon Jar back; Paddy his second Bamboo Brush, minimum 4, keeping the Wooden Bowl back; Graham his last Land Deed Scroll, minimum 6, keeping the Vase back. The Emperor doesn't bid.", map_hl=[5,6,7])
say("Silke", "Here's the second Customs House, as promised. Graham, it's got your name on it, so long as nobody goes back on their word. Four to open, same as last year.",
 "Silke: Paddy said publicly he'd stay out of this one, so Graham should get it cheap, and the deal holding means Paddy has to keep his hands off Graham's second Deed for me. I'm keeping the Dragon Jar back, because the last piece I sell is the one the table will want most.")
say("Paddy", "Here's the second Bamboo Brush, friends, at four. Silke said this one ought to go to Graham, and I'd be glad to see it go to him without a fight. Thank you for the Winter Palace, Reina. I'll gladly take water whenever the crown can spare it.",
 "Paddy: Graham lost the first brush to Silke, so putting the second one up now gives him a fair shot and keeps him friendly to me. The Wooden Bowl can wait until next year.")
say("Graham", "Last of the Scrolls, minimum six. Silke, it's yours to claim, and Paddy has told the whole table he's staying out. I'll take him at his word. I took him at his word last year too.",
 "Graham: Paddy offered me that Customs House cheap and then bid a grain over me for it, so this year I'm holding everyone to what they've said out loud. Silke wants this Scroll, and six only asks her to pay something near what she bid last time.")
say("Paddy", "I said I'd stay out, and I'm staying out. Graham, that Customs House is yours, and Silke, the Deed is yours, and I won't raise a finger on either. The Brush is still four if anyone wants it.",
 "Paddy: I already have those two pieces, so passing costs me nothing, and keeping my word now means Silke and Graham might trust me when the Jar and the Vase come up. Those are the two lots I'll really be fighting for.")
say("Silke", "Graham, the Brush is yours, I'm staying out of it. And Paddy, that Deed is the last one on the board, so if you've changed your mind about standing aside, say it now, to my face.",
 "Silke: That's the last Land Deed in the game, and without it I can't get to the throne, so I'll go to twenty if someone tries to block me. I'm leaving Graham's Brush alone, since fighting him for it would only fill the Harvest Pile.")
lot(5, "CH", [
 B(7, 4, "Thank you, Silke. Thank you, Paddy. I'll take you both at your word and bid four on each. Anyone who bids against me on the last copies is making a statement, and I'll remember it.",
   "Graham: I'm the only one who still needs these two, and they're the last copies on the table, so the ceilings sit well above the minimum. Nobody's going to keep my set from me for a few grain.")],
 hover=TH(("", "Nobody else bid; Graham would have gone to 20."),
          ("Graham", "It goes on S17.")), at="S17")
lot(6, "BB", [B(7, 4)],
 hover=TH(("", "Nobody else bid; Graham would have gone to 20."),
          ("Graham", "It goes on S18.")), at="S18")
lot(7, "LD", [B(5, 6)],
 hover=TH(("", "Nobody else bid; Silke would have gone to 20."),
          ("Silke", "It goes on P25.")), at="P25")
say("Graham", "Four and four. That's how it ought to go, friends. Thank you both. They're going down at the far end of the province, out of the way of any work the crown wants to build.",
 "Graham: That's two of the four, bought for eight grain, against the fourteen I nearly spent losing them last year. I put them out on the edge so no canal or road ever has a reason to sit on top of them.")
say("Silke", "The Deed goes down beside the Brush. Thank you, Graham, and thank you, Paddy, for keeping your word. Three deals honoured at one table, which must be a record for China.",
 "Silke: Six grain for the last Land Deed in the game is a bargain, and now I'm two pieces short of the set: Paddy's Bowl and Graham's Vase. They each hold the last copy of the piece I need, so from here on I'm the most polite woman on the coast.")
salaries({6:5}, text="Reina pays Paddy 5 for reading the I Ching.", hover="Nobody else at this table is on a salary.")

# ===== CHAPTER 2: The Set's Made to Be Used
# ============================== YEAR 3 ==============================
R(3, "The Wall is breached", "breach")
act("Paddy turns the I Ching card: The Wall Is Breached. Every province collects a good harvest. No Wall stands above Province 1, so Reina pays 2 in damages to the Harvest Pile instead of 5 for repairs (Provinces 2 and 3 are empty); Silke, Paddy and Graham each play the first personal disaster in their booklets (Provinces 4 and 8 are empty). The population holds at 100 million, x1.", map_hl=[6])
say("Paddy", "\"The Wall Is Breached.\" Good harvest for every province, so the barns are full, but horsemen have come through three links of the Great Wall. Provinces 1, 2 and 3 pay 5 million each for repairs, or 2 million into the Harvest Pile where no wall stands above them. Provinces 4 to 8 each play a Personal Disaster, and that's Silke, Graham and myself, so we each turn the next one in our booklets. Population is still 100 million, times one, so read the x1 row of your income tables. Sorry, Reina, the wall bill's yours. And sorry to the rest of us too.",
 "Paddy: A good harvest and a disaster for me in the same year. Whatever my booklet says, I'm still keeping twenty grain back, because the Jar and the Vase haven't come up yet and that's where my seventy-eight have to go.")
harvest("Graham pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile.")
act("The Wall Is Breached: no Wall stands above Province 1, so Reina pays 2 in damages to the Harvest Pile.", deltas={pid(1):-2}, pile_delta=2, map_hl=[1])
disaster(5, "bandits attack the Silk Peasant's family and are beaten off by a young warrior, and a shrine to him must be built.")
disaster(6, "the Rice Peasant's only water buffalo breaks its leg, and the planting stops until a new one is found.")
disaster(7, "the Emperor catches sight of the Wheat Peasant's eldest daughter on a trip to the capital and takes her into the imperial household as a maid to an imperial daughter, and by tradition he must give a feast for his relatives and friends.")
say("Reina", "Thirty again. Steady hands, steady house. The Wall bill's paid and the barns are full, so the water I promised starts flowing now: both sections of the canal in Province 5 this year, Silke. Paddy, yours is next year, both sections, and you have my word on it in front of everyone.",
 "Reina: Sixty in, eighty out, and Paddy's five comes off as well, so the reserve drops to about one-thirty-three. That's still enough for land when the population doubles, and now Silke's harvest pays me back ten more a section every year. Doing the whole canal at once, and naming Paddy's year out loud, keeps either of them from feeling like they're the one being kept waiting.")
taxes(.30, collector="Silke collects the tax for the crown")
build("Grand Canal", 80, {1:80}, canal={5:2}, text="Public works: Reina digs both sections of the Grand Canal in Province 5, Silke's, at the crown's cost of 80. Each adds 10 to Silke's harvest from next year.", hover="The 80 goes into the Harvest Pile. The Winter Palace, which the canals in Provinces 5 and 6 need first, already stands. 360 of the works are still owed.")
act("The auctions. Each seller has one piece left, the only one of its kind, and must put it up: Silke the Dragon Jar, minimum 15, which Paddy and Graham both need; Paddy the Wooden Bowl, minimum 2, which Silke and Graham both need; Graham the Vase, minimum 10, which Silke and Paddy both need. Each peasant is two pieces short of a set. The Emperor doesn't bid.", map_hl=[5,6,7])
say("Silke", "Thank you, Majesty. Two canal sections in Province 5, and I'll remember who dug them. Gentlemen, the Jar goes up first, and it's the only one in the world. I'll be honest: I'd rather it went to whichever of you lets me have my Bowl or my Vase cheaply than to whoever shouts loudest. Every grain we throw at each other goes into the Harvest Pile and helps none of us.",
 "Silke: Paddy and Graham each need my Jar, and I need one piece from each of them, so whoever wins the Jar has less left to fight me with. I've capped both of my bids at under forty, so even if I win both lots I keep enough for the twenty-grain set fee and a little left over.")
lot(5, "DJ", [
 B(6, 45, "Silke's right, and I'll say it plainly so nobody's guessing. My Bowl goes up at the minimum, 2 grain, so whoever needs it isn't held to ransom by me. I need the Jar and the Vase as much as anyone, though, so I'll bid what they're worth to me and stop there. If Graham beats me to the Jar, I'll shake his hand and that's that.",
   "Paddy: Each ceiling leaves me over twenty grain if I win both, so I can't overreach whatever happens. Graham's got the deepest pockets for the Jar, so I'm keeping an eye on him, but I won't pay double just to spite a neighbour."),
 B(6, None, "That's past what it's worth to me. It's yours, and well bought."),
 B(7, 46, "Paddy, I heard you, and I'll take that handshake on the same terms: I'll bid what the Jar is worth to me and not a grain past it. The Vase goes up at ten, which is fair for the only one in the world, and I'll ask nobody to thank me for it.",
   "Graham: If I take the Jar and the Bowl and still have the twenty spare, the set is mine this year, and I've done the sums so that even at my top price I keep twenty-five and a half. Last year I lost fourteen grain being polite, so this year I'm being exact.")],
 hover=TH(("", "Paddy's limit was 45. Graham would have gone to 60, and to 70 to keep it from Paddy."),
          ("Graham", "It goes on S16.")), at="S16")
lot(6, "WB", [
 B(5, 38), B(5, None, "Too rich for a silk farmer this year. Enjoy it, and mind you don't go hungry carrying it home."),
 B(7, 39)],
 hover=TH(("", "Silke's limit was 38; Graham would have gone to 50."),
          ("Graham", "It goes on S15.")), at="S15")
lot(7, "VA", [
 B(5, 36), B(5, None, "Too rich for a silk farmer this year. Enjoy it, and mind you don't go hungry carrying it home."),
 B(6, 37)],
 hover=TH(("", "Silke's limit was 36; Paddy would have gone to 45."),
          ("Paddy", "It goes on Q21.")), at="Q21")
say("Graham", "Paddy, here's my hand, as promised. Silke, thank you for the Bowl's company. That makes four pieces in a row on the S line, and I'll leave the table to count what that means.",
 "Graham: Forty-six and thirty-nine, well under my limits, so I've got the Jar, the Bowl, the Customs House, the Brush and about seventy grain, and the set is complete. Now I'm watching Silke and Paddy, because one of them is about to find out that the Wheat Peasant doesn't stay a peasant.")
say("Paddy", "The Vase goes on Q21, next to my Deed. Graham, that's the Jar and the Bowl both, so you've got the whole set, and I said I'd shake your hand, so here it is. Fairly won. Silke, I'm sorry it came down to the two of us on the Vase. I had to have it.",
 "Paddy: With the only Jar sitting in Graham's province, my set can't be finished at auction now, so the Vase is something I hold rather than something that finishes anything. That makes Graham the man to watch at this table from here on, and I mean to keep my grain for land and my people.")
act("Graham holds the Wheat Peasant's whole set, a Customs House, a Bamboo Brush, the Dragon Jar and the Wooden Bowl, and 69.5 grain, so he may replace another peasant (Section 11). Only the Silk Peasant may take the throne at this table.", map_hl=[7])
say("Graham", "Silke, in Year One you offered me the Brush and then outbid me by a grain. I wrote it down. The Reservoir's yours now, twenty a year, and I hope it treats you as well as it treated me. Majesty, I'll look after your two canal sections in Province 5 properly.",
 "Graham: I'm swapping the Reservoir's twenty for her canals' twenty and taking 190 grain, and the Silk Peasant is the only seat at this table that leads to the throne. All I need now is a Deed and Paddy's Vase, and I'll be counting every grain until I have them.")
swap(7, 5, "Graham Garner replaces Silke Weber as the Silk Peasant of Province 5. He takes her 120.5 grain with his own. The Dragon Jar, native to Province 5, goes back into its stock, for Graham to auction; his Customs House, Bamboo Brush and Wooden Bowl go on P24, P23 and P22. Silke returns her Bamboo Brush and Land Deed Scroll, draws 60 from the Harvest Pile and becomes the Wheat Peasant of Province 7, with its Reservoir.",
     hover="Graham: 69.5 + 120.5 = 190. Silke's Brush goes to Province 6's stock and her Deed to Province 7's, the provinces that started with them (Provinces 3 and 4 are empty). As the Silk Peasant, Graham still needs a Land Deed Scroll and the Vase; with them he may take the throne. Silke, as the Wheat Peasant, needs a Customs House, a Bamboo Brush, the Dragon Jar and the Wooden Bowl.",
     at={"CH":"P24","BB":"P23","WB":"P22"})
salaries({6:5}, text="Reina pays Paddy 5 for reading the I Ching.", hover="Nobody else at this table is on a salary.")

# ============================== YEAR 4 ==============================
R(4, "Drought", "blight")
act("Paddy turns the I Ching card: Drought. Every province collects an average harvest, and nothing else happens. The population holds at 100 million, x1.", map_hl=[6])
say("Paddy", "\"Drought. Not a cloud in months. The locusts, at least, are having a fine year.\" All provinces collect an average harvest, and nobody plays a disaster this time, thank heaven. The population is still 100 million, times one, so read the x1 row again. Welcome to Province 7, Silke. I hope the Reservoir does right by you.",
 "Paddy: Strange how it's gone: I've got three of my four pieces, and the one I'm missing, the Jar, is up for auction from Graham's stock this very year. He'll know how much I want it, so I've got to work out what it's really worth to me and still keep my twenty grain back.")
harvest("Graham pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile.")
say("Reina", "Thirty, same as every year. Nobody at this table can say I've squeezed them. Paddy, I gave you my word last year in front of everyone, so both sections of your canal go in this year and the crown pays all of it. Graham, congratulations on Province 5, and on its water. You're the richest at the table now, and I'll be watching that Land Deed Scroll auction with real interest.",
 "Reina: Keeping my word to Paddy costs me eighty and brings the reserve down to about a hundred and six. But Paddy is sitting on the one Vase Graham needs for the throne, and I'd like him to feel he owes the crown something before Graham starts making offers.")
taxes(.30, collector="Silke collects the tax for the crown")
build("Grand Canal", 80, {1:80}, canal={6:2}, text="Public works: Reina digs both sections of the Grand Canal in Province 6, Paddy's, at the crown's cost of 80, as she promised him last year. Each adds 10 to Paddy's harvest from next year.", hover="The 80 goes into the Harvest Pile. The Grand Canal in Provinces 5 and 6 is finished; the two sections in Province 1 wait on the Summer Palace. 280 of the works are still owed.")
act("The auctions. Each seller has one piece in stock and must put it up: Graham the Dragon Jar, minimum 60, the last piece Paddy needs and one Silke needs too; Paddy a Bamboo Brush, minimum 2, which Silke needs; Silke a Land Deed Scroll, minimum 60, which Graham needs. The Emperor doesn't bid.", map_hl=[5,6,7])
say("Graham", "The Jar's the last one in the world and it finishes a set, so it goes up at sixty. Anyone who thinks that's dear can wait a year and see what it costs then. Silke, I'll want your Deed, and I'll pay a fair price for it, more than a fair one if I have to.",
 "Graham: A finished set for Paddy means he can sit in my chair and take my 228, so I'd rather keep the Jar off the market for a year than sell him the key to my own barn. The Deed is the only piece I can actually buy, so I'll pay well for it, and more if the crown starts bidding against me.")
act("A lot that goes unsold must go up again the next year with its minimum at 2.")
lot(5, "DJ", [
 B(6, 60, "Graham, sixty's steep and you know it, but it's the last piece I need, so I'll meet you there and not run off into the clouds after it. The Brush goes up at 2 again, same as the Bowl did. Silke, it's yours for a song if you want it. And Majesty, thank you for the canal. You said you'd dig it and you did.",
   "Paddy: If I win the Jar I've got the whole set, and the canal will be watering Province 6 from next year whatever I end up doing with it. Seventy-five is my limit, which still leaves me over forty grain, and I'll leave the Deed to Graham and Silke to settle between them.")],
 hover=TH(("", "Nobody else bid; Paddy would have gone to 75."),
          ("Paddy", "It goes on Q22.")), at="Q22")
lot(6, "BB", [
 B(7, 2, "Well. Year One gets written down at this table and paid back in Year Three. I'll remember that, Graham, I've a long memory of my own. The Deed goes up at sixty, the same price you put on the Jar, so you can hardly call it dear. Paddy, thank you for the Brush at two. That's the first kind price I've had all night.",
   "Silke: I've gone from two pieces short of the throne to four short of anything, and the only card I hold now is the one Deed Graham needs, so he pays me his own price for it. I'm staying out of the Jar fight and buying the cheap Brush, because the Jar is the man's last piece and I can't win it. (Silke whispers to Paddy: \"Graham's one Vase short of the throne now, and the only one is sitting on your board. If you won't sell him the way up, I'll back you on anything you want at this table.\" The Storyteller reminds Paddy that Graham still needs a Land Deed Scroll as well as the Vase.)")],
 hover=TH(("", "Nobody else bid; Silke would have gone to 12."),
          ("Silke", "It goes on S18.")), at="S18")
lot(7, "LD", [B(5, 60)],
 hover=TH(("", "Nobody else bid; Graham would have gone to 90."),
          ("Graham", "It goes on P21.")), at="P21")
say("Paddy", "The Jar goes on Q22, in a row with the others: Customs House, Deed, Vase and Jar. I didn't think I'd ever say it, but that's my whole set. Graham, you'll understand the Vase isn't going anywhere now, since it's part of what I hold, and I'd sooner everyone heard me say that than have any of you hoping otherwise.",
 "Paddy: Silke offered to back me in a whisper, but I'd rather say where the Vase stands out loud, and it stays with me. Now I've got a full set and the grain to go with it, and I'm only just starting to think about what to do with that.")
say("Silke", "S18, where Graham's Brush used to stand. Two grain for it, and it's the first thing I've owned in Province 7. Graham, enjoy the Deed. Fifteen of that sixty is mine, so I'll thank you for the price you named.",
 "Silke: Graham has the Deed now and one piece, the Vase, between him and the throne, and the only Vase is on Paddy's board. Paddy has his whole set, so who does what next matters more than my two-grain Brush.")
say("Graham", "Sixty for sixty, Silke. That's fair, and I'll write it down as fair. Paddy, you've got your set now, so I'll shake your hand the way you shook mine, and I'll ask you to remember who sold you that Jar without a fight.",
 "Graham: That leaves me only the Vase short of the throne, and it's sitting on Paddy's Q21. The trouble is that Paddy's set is finished as of tonight, so I'll be watching where he decides to sit.")
act("Paddy holds the Rice Peasant's whole set, a Customs House, a Land Deed Scroll, the Vase and the Dragon Jar, and 58.5 grain, so he may replace another peasant (Section 11). Graham, the Silk Peasant, needs only the Vase to take the throne.", map_hl=[6])
say("Paddy", "Graham, I won't pretend this is easy to do to the man who sold me the Jar without a fight, and I'll not forget that. But the set's made to be used, and I'd be a fool to sit on it. I'm taking the Silk Peasant's seat. Province 6 is yours now, and Reina's canal goes with it, so you'll have good water to start on. And the Jar goes up from Province 5 at the fairest price I can put on it.",
 "Paddy: Graham was one Vase short of the throne and I was holding it, so by taking his seat I end that race, and I'm the only one left on the road to the crown with a Brush and a Bowl still to find. I'm sorry to do it to him, and I'm going to be very careful with his grain.")
swap(6, 5, "Paddy Doyle replaces Graham Garner as the Silk Peasant of Province 5. He takes Graham's 183.5 grain with his own. The Dragon Jar, native to Province 5, goes back into its stock, for Paddy to auction; his Customs House, Land Deed Scroll and Vase go on P24, P23 and P22. Graham returns his Customs House, Bamboo Brush, Wooden Bowl and Land Deed Scroll, draws 60 from the Harvest Pile and becomes the Rice Peasant of Province 6, with its two new canal sections.",
     hover="Paddy: 58.5 + 183.5 = 242. Graham's Customs House goes to Province 5's stock, his Brush and the Wooden Bowl to Province 6's, and his Deed to Province 7's (Note 12: the provinces that started with them, or their own). As the Silk Peasant, Paddy still needs a Bamboo Brush and the Wooden Bowl; with them he may take the throne. Graham, as the Rice Peasant, needs a Customs House, a Land Deed Scroll, the Dragon Jar and the Vase.",
     at={"CH":"P24","LD":"P23","VA":"P22"})
salaries({5:5}, text="Reina pays Paddy 5 for reading the I Ching.", hover="Nobody else at this table is on a salary.")

# ===== CHAPTER 3: Paid as I Said I Would
R(5, "The population doubles", "double1")
act("Paddy turns the I Ching card: Population Doubles. Every province collects a good harvest, and the population doubles to 200 million, x2, so every province eats twice what it did. With the population doubled, new land is open (Section 13): 20 a square, yielding 20 a year from the next harvest, and the Emperor must pay part of every square.", map_hl=[5])
say("Paddy", "\"Population Doubles.\" Good harvest for every province this year, but the counter goes from one hundred million to two hundred million, so we're all on the x2 row now. Everyone's consumption doubles from here, so check your tables before you count your grain. And if it comes round again we'll be at x4, so anyone running thin, Graham, I'm looking at you kindly, ought to think about new land while it's twenty a square.",
 "Paddy: A good harvest this year, but every mouth to feed counts double from now on, and Graham's down to sixty with my Brush and Bowl in his stock. I'd sooner he stayed on his feet and sold them to me fair than went under and they got tangled up in somebody's debts.")
harvest("Graham pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile.")
say("Reina", "Two hundred million mouths, friends, and mine is the hungriest province at this table, so I'll be straight with you: forty percent this year, sixteen grain each out of a forty-grain harvest. That money goes into the Summer Palace, which opens the Wall and the last canals, into three squares under the Wall so the crown stops eating your tax just to feed itself, and into a share of every square any of you buys. Five a square from me, as many squares as you want. Paddy, you warned us about the next card. I'm doing what you said.",
 "Reina: That isn't greed, it's arithmetic: a crown that loses forty-five a year can't pay for anyone's land, and then the whole table can call revolt on me. Three squares now gets me back to even, and once that's done the tax comes back down. Graham is the one I'm watching, because a subsidised square matters most to the man with the thinnest purse. (Reina whispers to Graham: \"You've got a hundred grain and a Bowl that Paddy wants badly. Buy land with it before you sell him anything, and if you take three squares this year I'll pay ten on each instead of five. The crown remembers its friends.\")")
taxes(.40, collector="Silke collects the tax for the crown")
build("Summer Palace", 20, {1:20}, key="Summer Palace", at="G18", text="Public works: Reina builds the Summer Palace on G18, in Province 1, and the crown pays the 20. It must stand before the Wall above Province 1 and the Grand Canal there.", hover="The 20 goes into the Harvest Pile. 260 of the works are still owed.")
buy_land(1, ["F23", "F24", "F25"], {1:60}, "New land: Reina buys three squares of sea for Province 1, along the coast at F23, F24 and F25, and the crown pays the 60. The strip below the Wall opens to Province 1 only once all four links above it stand.", "The 60 goes into the Harvest Pile. Each square adds 20 to Province 1's harvest from next year.")
act("The crown offers to pay 5 of every square the provinces buy this year. Paddy, Graham and Silke each ask for land, ten squares in all, and Graham, holding a whispered promise of 10 a square, asks the crown for 30 on his three. Paddy puts up a Customs House from Province 5's stock, minimum 2, and keeps the Dragon Jar back; Graham puts up a Bamboo Brush from Province 6's, minimum 70, and keeps the Wooden Bowl back.", map_hl=[5,6,7])
say("Paddy", "The Customs House goes up this year, minimum two, same as always, so nobody's held to ransom by me. And I'll take four squares of land along the coast, Reina. I'll pay fifteen a square and gladly take your five, since I'm the one who warned everybody about the next card and I'd look a fool not to do it myself.",
 "Paddy: Four squares covers me if the population card turns up again at x4, and I can still buy the Brush and the Bowl off Graham with plenty to spare. I'm keeping the Dragon Jar back for now, because a Jar on the block could hand somebody the last piece of a set before I've got mine.")
say("Graham", "The Brush goes up this year, minimum seventy. Paddy, you told me you wouldn't forget that I didn't fight you over the Jar, so you'll understand why I'm fighting now. The Bowl waits for a later year. And Reina, I'll take three squares of the south coast. You said you'd do what Paddy told you, and so will I.",
 "Graham: Paddy needs the Brush and the Bowl, I'm the only one selling either, and he only gets one a year, so every year he waits costs him more than it costs me. Meanwhile I'm buying three fields at the crown's expense so the next population card doesn't starve me first. (Graham whispers to Reina: \"Three squares, ten from the crown on each, the way you offered. I'll remember who paid for my fields, and so will the Bowl's minimum when it goes up.\")")
say("Silke", "Three squares, Reina, and I'll take your five on each. That's fifteen grain from the crown for land that feeds the crown's tax next year, so we both come out ahead. Paddy, you're the one who warned us about the next card, so I'd like to see you buy as much as you told us to.",
 "Silke: That's forty-five grain for sixty a year, and if that x4 card turns up I won't be the one starving at this table. I've kept ninety in hand on purpose, because Graham's Bowl comes up at auction and I'm not losing it by one grain twice.")
act("The crown is asked for 65 and holds 54.5, with Paddy's 5 for the I Ching still to pay at the end of the year. A share of a square is a voluntary payment, so it can't take the crown into debt.", map_hl=[1])
say("Reina", "Five a square, as I said in front of all of you, and I'll stand by it to the last grain, which is very nearly where it leaves me. Ten new fields, and that's ten more harvests the crown can tax next year. Graham, your third square is half a grain short of five; the crown will make that up and more once the coast starts growing.",
 "Reina: I promised five a square in public and ten to Graham in private, and the crown's purse can only cover the promise everyone heard. Graham gets the bad news quietly from me, along with an IOU, because a sore Graham holding the Bowl is the most dangerous thing at this table. (Reina whispers to Graham: \"I can't pay the ten this year: three of you asked at once and the crown is down to the salary. The fifteen and a half I owe you comes first out of next year's tax, before anything else is built. I'd rather be late in paying you than break my word to the whole table.\")")
buy_land(5, ["K25", "K26", "L26", "M26"], {5:60, 1:20}, "New land: Paddy buys four squares of sea for Province 5, at K25, K26, L26 and M26, paying 60, the crown paying 20.")
buy_land(7, ["T16", "T17", "T18"], {7:45, 1:15}, "New land: Silke buys three squares of the Warlord's desert for Province 7, at T16, T17 and T18, paying 45, the crown paying 15.")
say("Graham", "All three, then. Forty-five and a half from me, fourteen and a half from the crown, and I've written down the rest of what was said. A field is worth more than a grain in the jar when the next card comes.",
 "Graham: The crown promised me ten a square and paid me less than five, so Reina now owes me fifteen and a half, and I've written it down next to Paddy's name. I'll still take the fields, because four more squares is what stands between me and starving at x4, and I can't wait for anyone's promise to come good. (Graham whispers to Reina: \"Fifteen and a half, first out of next year's tax, before anything is built. I'll hold you to the exact figure.\")")
buy_land(6, ["R25", "R26", "S26"], {6:45.5, 1:14.5}, "New land: Graham buys three squares of sea for Province 6, at R25, R26 and S26, paying 45.5, the crown paying 14.5 (5, 5 and 4.5).")
act("The auctions. Paddy puts up a Customs House, minimum 2, which Graham and Silke both need; Graham the Bamboo Brush, minimum 70, the one Paddy needs; Silke a Land Deed Scroll, minimum 2, which Graham needs. Silke already has a Bamboo Brush on S18, so Graham's is the only one for sale. The Emperor doesn't bid.", map_hl=[5,6,7])
lot(5, "CH", [
 B(6, 18, "The Brush stays at seventy, as I said. Paddy, it's the only one at this table and you know what it's for. Silke, the Customs House: we both need one and every grain we bid against each other goes three-quarters into the pile. If you stand aside for me this year, Paddy's Dragon Jar, when it comes up, is yours without a bid from me.",
   "Graham: I've thirty-eight and a half and two pieces to start a set with, so I'll go to eighteen for the Customs House and no further. The Deed should come cheap, since nobody else needs it. The Brush at seventy is a bill I'm sending Paddy for the Jar, and if he won't pay it this year it'll cost him another year without the throne."),
 B(6, None, "Too rich for a man starting over. Take it, and I'll remember the price."),
 B(7, 19, "Graham, you've thirty-eight and a half grain and a long memory. I've ninety and a longer one. The Customs House is mine this year, and I'll put my Deed up at two so you're not shut out of the one piece you need.",
   "Silke: Graham can't bid past thirty-eight and a half, so forty buys the Customs House outright. Chasing Paddy's Brush past seventy against two hundred grain would just pour my grain into the Pile, so I'm saving it for when the Bowl comes up.")],
 hover=TH(("", "Graham's limit was 18; Silke would have gone to 40."),
          ("Silke", "It goes on S17.")), at="S17")
lot(6, "BB", [
 B(5, 70, "Seventy for a Brush, Graham. You've earned the right to ask it and I'll not haggle in front of everyone, I'll pay it. The Customs House stays at two like I said, and I'm keeping out of Silke's Deed. That's Graham's piece, and I'd like him to have it.",
   "Paddy: Most of Graham's seventy goes into the Harvest Pile and not to Graham, so a fight over it would only make us both poorer. I've given myself a bit of room above seventy in case anyone bids just to stop me, and the Bowl's the only piece left after this.")],
 hover=TH(("", "Nobody else bid; Paddy would have gone to 90."),
          ("Paddy", "It goes on P21.")), at="P21")
lot(7, "LD", [B(6, 2)],
 hover=TH(("", "Nobody else bid; Graham would have gone to 12."),
          ("Graham", "It goes on R22.")), at="R22")
say("Silke", "Nineteen grain, Graham. You offered me the Jar to step aside, and I'd rather own the Customs House than be promised something. It goes on S17, beside my Brush. Paddy, you've a Brush now as well, so I'll be watching that Bowl very closely.",
 "Silke: Paddy now has everything except the Bowl, and I'm two pieces short, so Graham's Bowl is the most dangerous thing on this board. Whoever Graham sells it to decides who gets the next seat.")
say("Paddy", "Seventy paid and gladly, Graham. The Brush goes on P21, next to the Vase. Silke, well bid on the House, and Graham, I'm glad you got your Deed for two.",
 "Paddy: Now there's only the Bowl left, and it's sitting in Graham's stock, so next year he holds all the cards and he knows it. I'm keeping 145 in hand for that day, and I mean to pay him fair rather than fight.")
say("Graham", "Two grain for a Deed. Thank you, Silke, that was fairly done. Paddy, seventy paid without a word. I'll write that down too, on the other side of the page.",
 "Graham: One piece on the board, fifty-four in the jar, and Paddy is now one Bowl short of the throne, a Bowl only I can sell him. Next year I'll name the price, and I'll be precise about it.")
salaries({5:5}, text="Reina pays Paddy 5 for reading the I Ching.", hover="Nobody else at this table is on a salary. It leaves the crown with nothing in reserve.")
R(6, "The Emperor's share", "share")
act("Paddy turns the I Ching card: The Emperor's Share. Every province collects an average harvest, and each province with a public work pays the Emperor half of what the work adds to its harvest this year. The population holds at 200 million, x2. Last year's new land yields its first 20 a square.", map_hl=[5])
say("Paddy", "\"The Emperor's Share.\" It's an average harvest everywhere, and any province with a public work splits the extra it grows half and half with the Emperor this round. The population holds at two hundred million, so we stay on the x2 row. Reina, it seems the ditches remember who dug them, and after last year I'd say you've earned it.",
 "Paddy: Reina's down to nothing, so this card puts a bit of grain back in the crown's purse, and I'd rather that than a broke Emperor closing the land to all of us. Next comes Graham and the Bowl, and that's the only thing left between me and the throne.")
harvest("Graham pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile.")
act("The Emperor's Share: Paddy's two canal sections, Graham's two and Silke's Reservoir each add 20 to their harvests, so each pays Reina 10. New land isn't a public work, so its harvest isn't shared.", "Province 1 has no work that adds to its harvest yet.",
    {pid(5):-10, pid(6):-10, pid(7):-10, pid(1):30}, map_hl=[1,5,6,7])
act("Reina owes Graham 15.5, promised in a whisper last year in place of the 10 a square she'd offered him for his land, first out of this year's tax.", map_hl=[1,6])
say("Reina", "Graham, fifteen and a half, counted out in front of everyone and before a single stone is laid, exactly as I said. Forty percent again, friends: that's thirty-eight from Paddy and thirty each from Graham and Silke. It pays for the first canal under the Wall, two more fields for the hungriest province at the table, and five a square toward any land you buy, same as last year. You saw what the last card did. The next one is x4, and I'd like us all to have our feet under us before it comes.",
 "Reina: Last year I ran the crown down to five grain, and that's a mistake you only get to make once. The fields go first because each square pays for itself in a year, and I build one canal section rather than two so there's still something in the jar when the x4 card turns over.")
taxes(.40, collector="Silke collects the tax for the crown")
act("Reina pays Graham the 15.5 the crown owed him.", "A promise kept: \"first out of next year's tax, before anything else is built\" (Year 5).", {pid(1):-15.5, pid(6):15.5}, map_hl=[1,6], deal=True)
build("Grand Canal", 40, {1:40}, canal={1:1}, text="Public works: Reina digs the first section of the Grand Canal in Province 1, at the crown's cost of 40. It adds 10 to Province 1's harvest from next year.", hover="The 40 goes into the Harvest Pile. 220 of the works are still owed: the second canal section in Province 1, the four Wall links above it and the Marble Pagoda.")
buy_land(1, ["F26", "F27"], {1:40}, "New land: Reina buys two more squares of sea for Province 1, at F26 and F27, and the crown pays the 40. The crown offers to pay 5 of every square the provinces buy this year.")
say("Paddy", "Not this year, Reina, thank you. My four squares cover me even if the x4 card comes, so keep your five a square for Graham and Silke and your own coast. You've stretched the crown far enough for us already.",
 "Paddy: My card says I'm covered at x4, and every grain I hold now is for the Bowl. Graham knows it's the last piece I need and Silke says she's watching it, so I want to be able to pay what it takes when it comes up.")
say("Graham", "Fifteen and a half, paid in full and first, as promised. Reina, I'd written it down and now I've crossed it out. Two more squares of the coast, please, at your five each.",
 "Graham: One square covers me if the x4 card comes, and the second is a margin, because last time I counted to the exact grain I was the one who got caught. That still leaves seventy-odd in the jar for Paddy's Jar, and Paddy has to come to me for the Bowl whatever he bids for it.")
buy_land(6, ["Q26", "R27"], {6:30, 1:10}, "New land: Graham buys two more squares of sea for Province 6, at Q26 and R27, paying 30, the crown paying 10.")
say("Silke", "Two more squares, Reina, at your five apiece. The desert's starting to look like a farm. Paddy, you've every piece but the Bowl and two hundred grain behind you, so Graham, I'd think very hard about who you sell it to.",
 "Silke: Two squares cover me if the x4 card comes, and I keep seventy-seven in hand for the Bowl or the Jar. Paddy is one piece from a seat now, and I mean to keep reminding Graham whose seat that's likely to be.")
buy_land(7, ["T15", "T19"], {7:30, 1:10}, "New land: Silke buys two more squares of the Warlord's desert for Province 7, at T15 and T19, paying 30, the crown paying 10.")
act("The auctions, the last two pieces in stock. Paddy puts up the Dragon Jar, minimum 2, which Graham and Silke both need; Graham the Wooden Bowl, minimum 120, the last piece Paddy needs, and Silke needs it too. The Emperor doesn't bid.", map_hl=[5,6,7])
say("Paddy", "I'll be plain about it: I want that Bowl, and I've the grain to outlast anyone at this table for it. Silke, every grain we push up goes three-quarters into the pile, so I'd ask you to stand aside. The Jar goes up first at two and I'll not touch it, and whatever I do with the throne, I'll treat you fairly after. You offered to back me once, and I haven't forgotten.",
   "Paddy: Silke has 77 and I have 197, so a war over the Bowl only throws her grain into the Harvest Pile and gets her nothing. I've set my ceiling well above anything she can reach, and I still keep the 20 I need to take the throne.")
lot(5, "DJ", [
 B(6, 45, "The Bowl goes up at a hundred and twenty. Paddy, you've told this table twice now that you'll pay what I ask and won't haggle, and this is what I'm asking: the last piece, the only one, and the throne behind it. Silke, the Jar's between you and me, and I'll go as far as I can afford.",
   "Graham: Paddy can pay a hundred and twenty, and if he does, thirty of it comes back to me and he's poorer than a man taking the throne would want to be. If he balks, the rules put it up at two next round and he gets it for nothing, so I'm betting he wants it badly enough not to make the table watch him wait."),
 B(7, 45, "Graham, think about who's at the other end of that Bowl. Sell it to Paddy and he's finished, and the first seat he looks at is a coastal one with five fresh fields. I'll be bidding every grain I have on it, so if he wants it, he'll pay dearly.",
   "Silke: I can't outbid two hundred grain, but I can make Paddy pay every bit of it, and three-quarters of the price goes into the Pile, not into a war chest for my seat. If the Jar comes cheap I'll take it. If not, Graham's welcome to it, and to the trouble that comes with it."),
 B(6, 52, "Fifty-two, and that's where I stop. Silke, every grain you spend on this Jar is a grain you can't spend making Paddy pay for the Bowl. I'd think about which of those you'd rather do.",
   "Graham: If Silke goes past fifty-two she'll have twenty-odd left to fight Paddy with, and he'll walk off with the Bowl at my minimum. Either I get the Jar, or she pays for it and Paddy's bill gets lighter. I can count either way, and I'd rather it was the first."),
 B(6, None, "That's my limit. I don't bid what I can't count back."),
 B(7, 53, "Seventy-five, Graham. That's every grain you've got and half a grain more. Year One cost me one grain and Year Three cost me two lots, and I've decided the Jar is where I stop paying for them.",
   "Silke: Graham can't go past seventy-four and a half, so the Jar is mine, and he's still a piece short and no danger to my seat. I'll have hardly any grain left, but at this table a full granary only draws a knife. Paddy will get his Bowl at a hundred and twenty, though, and that's the next problem.")],
 hover=TH(("", "Both stopped at 45, so each had one raise: Graham to 52, Silke to 75. Silke pays one over Graham's limit."),
          ("Silke", "It goes on S16.")), at="S16")
lot(6, "WB", [B(5, 120)],
 hover=TH(("", "Silke had only 24 left, under the minimum; Paddy would have gone to 120 and no further."),
          ("Paddy", "It goes on P20.")), at="P20")
say("Silke", "S16, next to the Customs House and the Brush. Paddy, there's your set finished and very nearly your throne, and you'll notice I'm the poorest peasant at this table now, with twenty-four grain. Graham's the one with a hundred and four in his jar, if anyone's counting.",
 "Silke: I paid fifty-three and Graham collected thirty for his Bowl, so he's the fat one now and I'm too thin to be worth a knife. I'm one Wooden Bowl from my own set, and Paddy's moving up, so I'll be watching whose stock it ends up in.")
say("Paddy", "A hundred and twenty, Graham, paid as I said I would, and the Bowl goes on P20 with the rest. That's the whole set, and I'll not pretend I don't know what it means. Silke, I hear you about the coast, and I'd ask you to watch what I do with it before you decide what I'm going to do.",
 "Paddy: The set's finished and I've ninety and a half left, plenty over the twenty, and I've no wish to take the seat of a neighbour who just sold me what I needed. If I climb, it's the throne I'm looking at, and I'll want to be a fair Emperor to the people who got me there.")
act("Paddy holds the Silk Peasant's whole set, a Land Deed Scroll, the Vase, a Bamboo Brush and the Wooden Bowl (and a Customs House besides), and 90.5 grain. With no upper class at this table, the Silk Peasant may take the throne with a set, or replace another peasant (Section 11).", map_hl=[5])
say("Paddy", "I'll take the throne. Reina, you've been a better Emperor than this table had any right to expect: you dug my canal, you paid your share of every field, and you kept your word to the last grain. The coast is a good province and it'll feed you well. Graham, Silke, I'm not taking a neighbour's seat. I'll keep the tax honest and get on with the works we still owe.",
 "Paddy: Taking Graham's grain or Silke's would have meant robbing someone who just dealt fairly with me, so the throne's the only climb I can make with a clear conscience. It comes with the hungriest province on the board and 220 of works still owed, so now I find out whether being careful with every grain works for a whole empire.")
swap(5, 1, "Paddy Doyle takes the throne. He replaces Reina Osorio as Emperor, keeps his 90.5 and takes the crown's reserve of 27.5, and returns all five of his pieces to the stock beside the board. Reina draws 60 from the Harvest Pile and becomes the Silk Peasant of Province 5, with its two canal sections and four squares of new land.",
     hover="Paddy: 90.5 + 27.5 = 118. Becoming Emperor returns every piece (Section 11): the Customs House to Province 5's stock, the Bamboo Brush and the Wooden Bowl to Province 6's, the Land Deed Scroll and the Vase to Province 7's. As the Silk Peasant, Reina needs a Land Deed Scroll, the Vase, a Bamboo Brush and the Wooden Bowl; with them and 20 grain she may take the throne back. The table jobs are appointments and stay with the players until the Emperor changes them.")
say("Paddy", "Graham keeps the pile and Silke keeps the tax book. You've both done it honestly and I'll not fix what isn't broken. Reina, I'd like you to read the I Ching from now on. Five grain a year to each of you, paid every year, because a job that's done ought to be paid for, even when nobody owes it.",
 "Paddy: Fifteen grain out of 118 isn't much to keep the table on my side while I find my feet. Reina knows the throne better than anyone here, so I'd rather keep her close and paid than sitting on the coast with nothing to do but plot.")
act("The new Emperor's appointments: Graham still banks the Harvest Pile, Silke still collects the tax, and Reina reads the I Ching and keeps the population counter from next year.", map_hl=[1])
salaries({5:5, 6:5, 7:5}, text="Paddy pays Reina, Graham and Silke 5 each for their jobs.", hover="An appointee's pay is the Emperor's choice (Note 3); none is owed. Paddy says he'll pay it every year.")
# ===== CHAPTER 4: I've Written Mine Down Too
R(7, "The public utility tax", "utility")
act("Reina turns the I Ching card: Utility Tax. Every province collects an average harvest, every province pays the Emperor 2, and every province with a public work pays 2 more. The population holds at 200 million, x2.", map_hl=[5])
say("Reina", "Utility Tax: 'a new levy, for the upkeep of things you may or may not have.' An average harvest for every province, and population stays at times two, two hundred million, so read the x2 row. Then every province pays the Emperor 2, and 2 more if it has a Public Works. I built a Public Works in every one of the four provinces we live in, so by my count that's 4 each, and Paddy, you're paying yourself. You're welcome, all of you.",
 "Reina: The card is charging all of us for the canals and the reservoir I dug, and I can't say I mind. The table should remember who built what it's being taxed on, so when I come for the throne again there's a record that I kept my promises.")
harvest("Graham pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile.")
act("Utility Tax: Reina's canals, Graham's canals and Silke's Reservoir are public works, so each of Provinces 5, 6 and 7 pays Paddy 4. Province 1's levy is the crown paying itself.", "The Utility Tax isn't a harvest income, so it isn't taxed.",
    {pid(5):-4, pid(6):-4, pid(7):-4, pid(1):12}, map_hl=[1,5,6,7])
say("Paddy", "Thirty percent, same for everyone, and I'll not go higher while the crown can carry itself. I'm finishing the canal Reina started up in my own province, and I'll pay half of any square of new land any of you buys this year. Buy before the x4 card comes, please, I'd sooner subsidise you now than watch you go into debt later. Your five apiece is coming too, as promised.",
 "Paddy: The canal's last section adds ten a year, and those three squares are just the number the board says I'm short if the population quadruples, so I'm buying them while I can afford it. Paying half of everybody's land costs me something now, but I can't see a cheaper way to keep three hungry neighbours away from revolt.")
taxes(.30, collector="Silke collects the tax for the crown")
build("Grand Canal", 40, {1:40}, canal={1:1}, text="Public works: Paddy digs the second section of the Grand Canal in Province 1, at the crown's cost of 40, and the Grand Canal is finished. It adds 10 more to Province 1's harvest from next year.", hover="The 40 goes into the Harvest Pile. 180 of the works are still owed: the four Wall links above Province 1 and the Marble Pagoda.")
buy_land(1, ["E23", "E24", "E25"], {1:60}, "New land: Paddy buys three squares of sea for Province 1, beyond the coast at E23, E24 and E25, and the crown pays the 60. The crown offers to pay 10 of every square the provinces buy this year, half the cost.")
say("Reina", "Three squares, J25 to J27, and I'll take your half gladly, Paddy. That's 30 from me and 30 from the crown. It's nice being on this side of the subsidy. And thank you for finishing my canal. That's two of my promises the new crown has kept for me.",
 "Reina: Ten grain a square for twenty a year back is the best deal on the table, but I'm keeping almost a hundred in hand, because the Bowl and the Vase will go to auction soon and I mean to outbid for them.")
buy_land(5, ["J25", "J26", "J27"], {5:30, 1:30}, "New land: Reina buys three squares of sea for Province 5, at J25, J26 and J27, paying 30, the crown paying 30.")
say("Graham", "The Brush goes up this year, forty to open, and Reina, I'll say it before anyone else does: you're the one who needs it, so I've priced it for you and not for Paddy. The Bowl stays beside the board. And I'll take four squares on the crown's half, Paddy. Forty from me, forty from you. I've written your offer down, and this time it's in the right column.",
 "Graham: Silke has every piece but the Bowl and a hundred and five grain to bid with, and the nearest seat she could take is mine, so the Bowl stays on my side of the table until I've counted what she'd do with it. The Brush brings in grain from Reina, the land pays for itself in a year, and I don't have to give anything away to get either.")
buy_land(6, ["P27", "Q27", "Q28", "R28"], {6:40, 1:40}, "New land: Graham buys four squares of sea for Province 6, at P27, Q27, Q28 and R28, paying 40, the crown paying 40.")
say("Silke", "The Vase goes up, twenty to open, and it goes to whoever wants it. I've no favourites this year. And I'll take two squares on your half, Paddy, thank you. That's the tax book and the subsidy both kept honest by the same woman.",
 "Silke: I'm keeping the Deed back because I don't know yet whose set it finishes, and I'm not handing anyone a knife I can't see. Every grain the Vase brings in goes into the jar for the day Graham finally puts that Bowl on the table.")
buy_land(7, ["T14", "T20"], {7:20, 1:20}, "New land: Silke buys two more squares of the Warlord's desert for Province 7, at T14 and T20, paying 20, the crown paying 20.")
act("The auctions. Reina puts up her Customs House, minimum 25, which Graham needs; Graham the Bamboo Brush, minimum 40, which Reina needs; Silke the Vase, minimum 20, which Reina and Graham both need. The Emperor doesn't bid.", map_hl=[5,6,7])
say("Reina", "Graham, thank you. Forty for the Brush is a fair price and I'll pay it without haggling. The Vase is another matter: I'm in for it, and I'd rather we didn't feed the Harvest Pile by bidding each other up. My Customs House opens at twenty-five, and whoever buys it buys it from a woman who remembers favours.",
 "Reina: Graham's the one I'm watching. With my Customs House and Silke's Vase he'd be one step from a set and he has the most grain at the table, so I'm paying forty for my Brush and spending nearly everything else to keep the Vase out of his hands.")
say("Graham", "Forty for the Brush, as I said, and I'm glad you're not haggling, Reina. I'll take your Customs House at your price, and I'll owe you the favour. The Vase I want as well, I'll be honest about that. I'll go to forty and no further, so if you want it more than that it's yours, and the Pile gets nothing extra out of either of us.",
 "Graham: Nobody else needs the Customs House, so twenty-five should do it, and Reina will owe me for the Brush as well. On the Vase I've told the table my limit to the grain, because a war with Reina only pays the Harvest Pile, and there's no Jar in anyone's stock for me to finish the set with anyway.")
say("Silke", "I'm out of both of the first two. I've got my Brush and my Customs House and I won't pay twice for either. The Vase stays at twenty, as I said, and I'll be watching to see which of you thinks it's worth more than forty.",
 "Silke: Graham needs the Customs House and the Vase, and if he gets both he's holding a knife again. So I'm happy for Reina to outbid his forty, and every grain she spends on my Vase gives me a quarter toward the Bowl.")
lot(5, "CH", [B(6, 25)],
 hover=TH(("", "Nobody else bid; Graham would have gone to 30."),
          ("Graham", "It goes on R23.")), at="R23")
lot(6, "BB", [B(5, 40)],
 hover=TH(("", "Nobody else bid; Reina's limit was 40."),
          ("Reina", "It goes on O20.")), at="O20")
lot(7, "VA", [B(6, 40), B(6, None, "That's past my number. It's yours, and I've written down what it cost you."), B(5, 41)],
 hover=TH(("", "Graham's limit was 40; Reina would have gone to 50, or 55 to keep it from Graham."),
          ("Reina", "It goes on O21.")), at="O21")
say("Graham", "R23, next to the Deed. Thank you, Reina, twenty-five as you said. And forty-one for the Vase is one more than my number, which tells me you counted mine as closely as I count everyone else's. I've written that down to your credit.",
 "Graham: Two pieces down and the Vase gone to Reina for a grain over my limit, which leaves her with twenty-two and a half and in debt to nobody but the Pile. The Jar's on Silke's square and the Vase is in Reina's hands, so I'm counting which of them slips first, and I'm still holding the Bowl.")
say("Reina", "The Brush and the Vase, side by side on my own coast. Graham, thank you for stopping at forty and keeping your word on it, and I haven't forgotten I owe you for the Customs House. Two pieces down. The Deed and the Bowl are all I lack now.",
 "Reina: That one grain over Graham kept the Vase out of his set, and now I have two of my four. Silke holds the Deed and Graham the Bowl, so from here on the job is keeping both of them sweet.")
salaries({5:5, 6:5, 7:5}, text="Paddy pays Reina, Graham and Silke 5 each for their jobs, as he promised.", hover="An appointee's pay is the Emperor's choice (Note 3); none is owed. Paddy promised it every year (Year 6).")
R(8, "A careless year", "careless")
act("Reina turns the I Ching card: Careless Prosperity. Every province collects a good harvest, and every player plays the next personal disaster in their booklet. The population holds at 200 million, x2.", map_hl=[5])
say("Reina", "Careless Prosperity: 'Good times make careless people.' Every province collects a good harvest, and then every player plays a Personal Disaster, the next one in your booklet. Population holds at times two, two hundred million, so read the x2 row. Enjoy the harvest, and then turn your page carefully.",
 "Reina: The Wall and the Pagoda could both be done by next year, so my time is short. I need Silke's Deed and Graham's Bowl, and a good harvest plus everyone's bad luck is the best chance I'll get to pay for them.")
harvest("Graham pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile.")
disaster(1, "the Emperor's six-year-old Prince breaks the Empress's favourite ivory figure, five hundred years old, and new ones are promised.")
disaster(5, "the irrigation ditch the Silk Peasant and her sons built over the years is washed away by a flash flood, and must be rebuilt.")
disaster(6, "fire takes the Rice Peasant's house and grain reserve, and he borrows to get the family through the year.")
disaster(7, "the Wheat Peasant's blacksmith shop catches fire, and the furnace takes two months to fix.")
say("Paddy", "Thirty percent again. I said I'd not go higher while the crown could carry itself, and it still can. I'm raising the whole Wall this year, all four links, so that only the Pagoda's left. Same offer on land as last year, half of every square, and your five apiece is coming as usual. Reina, well done on the Brush and the Vase. I know how dear those last two pieces feel.",
 "Paddy: The Wall is the dear one, so I'm paying for it while the reserve can cover it, and that leaves the cheap Pagoda for next year to end the reign. Reina's only two pieces short, though, so I'm watching who sells her the Deed and the Bowl.")
taxes(.30, collector="Silke collects the tax for the crown")
build("Great Wall", 160, {1:160}, wall={1:4}, text="Public works: Paddy raises all four links of the Great Wall above Province 1, at the crown's cost of 160. The strip below the Wall is open to Province 1 for new land.", hover="The 160 goes into the Harvest Pile. Only the Marble Pagoda (20) is still owed; it is built in a year when nothing else is, and the game ends in survival the moment it stands.")
act("The crown offers to pay 10 of every square the provinces buy this year, half the cost, and buys none for Province 1.", map_hl=[1])
say("Reina", "No land for me this year, Paddy, kind as the offer is. And congratulations on the Wall, that's a promise I made in Year 1 that you've kept for me. Silke, Graham: a Deed and a Bowl are all I lack, and I'm holding my grain for them. If either of you puts one up, you'll find me a serious buyer and a grateful one.",
 "Reina: With only the Pagoda left the game could end next year, so a square of land is worth less to me than grain in hand for the Deed and the Bowl. I'm telling the whole table I'm saving, so Silke and Graham know there's money waiting if they sell.")
say("Graham", "Four more squares on your half, Paddy, the same as last year. Ten grain a square that comes back as twenty at the next harvest is the cheapest sum I'll do all year. Reina, I heard you. The Bowl's still in my stock and I haven't forgotten whose it would finish.",
 "Graham: Even if the Pagoda goes up next year and ends it, each of these squares pays me back double once, so I'm not going to argue with the sums. Reina needs the Bowl and so does Silke, and while it sits beside my board I'm the one person both of them have to come to.")
buy_land(6, ["O27", "O28", "P28", "S27"], {6:40, 1:40}, "New land: Graham buys four more squares of sea for Province 6, at O27, O28, P28 and S27, paying 40, the crown paying 40.")
say("Silke", "Three more squares of desert on your half, Paddy, and thank you. It's ten a square and it comes back twice that, and even I can't argue with sums like that. Reina, I heard you as well. My Deed's still in my stock, and I know who else at this table holds a piece somebody's waiting on.",
 "Silke: Reina wants my Deed and Graham's Bowl, and I want that same Bowl, so for once I'm holding the piece someone else has to come and ask for. The land's cheap insurance, because if the Pagoda goes up next year I want my jar full when the counting starts.")
buy_land(7, ["T12", "T13", "T21"], {7:30, 1:30}, "New land: Silke buys three more squares of the Warlord's desert for Province 7, at T12, T13 and T21, paying 30, the crown paying 30.")
act("The auctions, the last two pieces in stock, each its seller's last: Graham's Wooden Bowl, minimum 60, which Reina and Silke both need, and Silke's Land Deed Scroll, minimum 20, which Reina needs. The Emperor doesn't bid.", map_hl=[5,6,7])
say("Reina", "Here we are: two pieces, and two of us who want the same Bowl. Silke, you've more grain than I do, and we both know it. If you outbid me, you'll have earned it, and I'd ask only that you keep Paddy's thirty percent and his half on the land. But if the Bowl comes to me, I'm asking you to price your Deed for the woman who built your Reservoir.",
 "Reina: I can't outspend Silke's hundred and ninety, so I'm making her pay dearly for the throne if she wants it. If she flinches, I keep just enough back to buy her Deed and take my crown back.")
say("Graham", "The Bowl opens at sixty. It's the last one in the game and two of you need it, and sixty is a good deal less than Paddy paid me for it. After that it goes to whoever wants it most, and I've no favourites either, Silke. The Deed's none of my business. I've got one already.",
 "Graham: If Reina buys the Bowl and then the Deed she has the whole set, and the richest peasant at the table is sitting in the chair she'd be able to take. So I'll pay up to ninety for a Deed I don't need rather than hand her two hundred and forty grain and my seat.")
say("Silke", "Reina, you built my Reservoir and I haven't forgotten it, but this Bowl is the one piece I've been waiting on since Year Three, and I'm going to buy it. The Deed opens at twenty, which is the fair price for it and not a grain more, whoever ends up holding the Bowl.",
 "Silke: Reina has 143.5 and still has to buy my Deed, so she can't stay with me much past that, and at 165 I'd still have the twenty I need left over. Once the set's on my desert, there's a Rice Peasant sitting on 240 who once wrote down a single grain.")
lot(6, "WB", [B(5, 110), B(5, None, "Well bid. I'll be watching how you wear it."), B(7, 111)],
 hover=TH(("", "Reina's limit was 110; Silke would have gone to 165."),
          ("Silke", "It goes on S15.")), at="S15")
lot(7, "LD", [B(6, 90), B(6, None, "That's past my number. Take it, and I'll count what it cost you."), B(5, 91)],
 hover=TH(("", "Graham bid only to keep it from Reina, up to 90; Reina would have gone to 123."),
          ("Reina", "It goes on O22.")), at="O22")
say("Silke", "S15, right next to the Jar. A hundred and eleven grain, Graham, and I paid it to you without haggling, which I'd like you to write down. Reina, you fought fairly, and you've the Deed to show for it.",
 "Silke: The Bowl, the Jar, the Customs House and the Brush now sit in one row on my desert, and the set is finished. Graham has 267.5 grain and the seat he took from me in Year Three, and he's the one who taught me what a finished set is for.")
say("Reina", "O22, next to the Brush and the Vase. Graham, ninety for a Deed you told us was none of your business. I've noted that, the same way you note everything. And Silke, well bought. You've a Bowl now and I'm the province with the canals, so I'll be watching which coast you fancy.",
 "Reina: Three of four, and the last Bowl in the game just went to Silke, so the throne is shut to me unless she sells it. Worse, a full set lets her replace me, so the next few turns are about making sure she'd rather have Graham's rice than my silk.")
act("Silke holds the Wheat Peasant's whole set, a Customs House, a Bamboo Brush, the Dragon Jar and the Wooden Bowl, and 103 grain. A peasant with a set may replace another peasant (Section 11): Reina, the Silk Peasant, or Graham, the Rice Peasant.", map_hl=[7])
say("Silke", "Graham, you took my seat in Year Three over a single grain, and you wrote down your reason. I've written mine down too: one Brush, one Jar, and a hundred and eleven for your Bowl. I'll take the Rice seat, thank you, and the Wheat fields are yours. They're good desert, and I bought most of it myself.",
 "Silke: I'm taking the richest seat at the table off the man who taught me how it's done, and that's 370 grain in my jar, from the poorest peasant to the fattest in one move. Reina keeps her coast, so she's got no reason to come after me, and Graham can find out how the desert feeds a family.")
swap(7, 6, "Silke Weber replaces Graham Garner as the Rice Peasant of Province 6. She takes his 267.5 grain with her own. The Wooden Bowl, native to Province 6, goes back into its stock, for Silke to auction; her Customs House, Bamboo Brush and Dragon Jar go on R23, R22 and R24. Graham returns his Land Deed Scroll and Customs House, draws 60 from the Harvest Pile and becomes the Wheat Peasant of Province 7, with its Reservoir and ten squares of new land.",
     hover="Silke: 103 + 267.5 = 370.5. The Deed goes back to Province 7's stock and the Customs House to Province 5's (Note 12). As the Rice Peasant Silke needs a Land Deed Scroll and the Vase; as the Wheat Peasant Graham needs a Customs House, a Bamboo Brush, the Dragon Jar and the Wooden Bowl. The table jobs are appointments and stay with the players.",
     at={"CH":"R23", "BB":"R22", "DJ":"R24"})
salaries({5:5, 6:5, 7:5}, text="Paddy pays Reina, Silke and Graham 5 each for their jobs, as he promised.", hover="An appointee's pay is the Emperor's choice (Note 3); none is owed. Paddy promised it every year (Year 6). Reina reads the I Ching, Silke collects the tax and Graham banks the Harvest Pile, whatever their seats.")
# ===== CHAPTER 5: An Awful Lot of Fair Dealing
R(9, "Imperial charity", "chest")
act("Reina turns the I Ching card: Imperial Charity. Every province collects an average harvest, and the Emperor gives every player 2 from the crown's reserve. The population holds at 200 million, x2.", map_hl=[5])
say("Reina", "\"Imperial Charity. Moved by the suffering of his people, the Emperor opens his purse. Slightly.\" Average harvest everywhere, population stays at two hundred million, so read your times-two row, and Paddy owes each of us two from the reserve. Slightly is right, but I'll take it, Paddy. And Silke, an average year is a good year for talking about Bowls and Vases.",
 "Reina: An average harvest leaves everyone where they stand, and the clock keeps running, since the Pagoda is the only work left and the game ends the moment it goes up. I'm holding the Vase Silke needs and she's holding the Bowl I need, so I'm putting that on the table in front of everyone where it can't quietly go to someone else.")
harvest("Graham pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile.")
act("Imperial Charity: Paddy gives Reina, Silke and Graham 2 each from the crown's reserve.", "The card's gift comes from the Emperor's own reserve, not the Harvest Pile.",
    {pid(5):2, pid(6):2, pid(7):2, pid(1):-6}, map_hl=[1])
say("Paddy", "Thirty percent, same as I promised, and I'll pay half of any square you buy, same as always. And yes, the Pagoda goes up this year. It's the last stone Reina's canal was waiting on, and I'd sooner finish the reign honestly than sit on it. Thank you all for an awful lot of fair dealing.",
 "Paddy: The Pagoda's only twenty grain and it ends the game before anyone can bid on that Bowl, so I'm keeping the tax where I said it'd be and building it. I'm still a bit surprised it's me that gets to put the last stone on.")
taxes(.30, collector="Silke collects the tax for the crown")
build("Marble Pagoda", 20, {1:20}, key="Marble Pagoda", at="G19", text="Public works: Paddy builds the Marble Pagoda on G19, in Province 1, between the Summer and Winter Palaces, at the crown's cost of 20. It is the last work owed.", hover="The Emperor's booklet: in a round when nothing else is built, the project is the Marble Pagoda. The 20 goes into the Harvest Pile. With it standing, every work owed is built, and the game ends at once (Section 18): no land, no auctions, no salaries.")
act("Every public work owed at this table is standing: the three palaces, the four Wall links above Province 1, the Grand Canal's six sections in Provinces 1, 5 and 6, and the Province 7 Reservoir, 520 in all. The game ends here, in the ninth year, before the auctions: the dynasty has survived. Each player's result is the seat they hold against the seat they drew. Paddy drew the Rice Peasant (6) and ends on the throne: a win. Graham drew the Wheat Peasant (7) and ends in it: a hold. Silke drew the Silk Peasant (5) and ends as the Rice Peasant (6): a loss. Reina drew the Emperor (1) and ends as the Silk Peasant (5): a loss.",
    "Section 18: a lower seat than the one drawn is a win, the same seat a hold, a higher seat a loss; the Emperor still on the throne has won. Final grain: Silke 570, Graham 217.5, Reina 168, the crown 454. The Wooden Bowl Reina needed never came to auction.",
    map_hl=[1,5,6,7], big=True)

RESULT = export(
 "Every public work owed was built in Year 9, so the dynasty survived (Section 18). Players are ranked by the seat they finished in against the one they drew; an Emperor still on the throne has won.",
 ["Reina drew the throne and built a work every year of her reign: the Province 7 Reservoir before any palace, the Winter Palace, both canal sections in Provinces 5 and 6, the Summer Palace and Province 1's first canal section. In Year 5 she bought the crown's first land and paid five a square towards everyone else's, which left the reserve at nothing. Paddy took the throne from her in Year 6. On the Silk seat she bought the Brush, the Vase and the Deed, but lost the last Wooden Bowl to Silke by one grain, so she ended one piece short of the throne she drew.",
  "Silke drew the Silk seat and lost it in Year 3, when Graham outbid her for the Jar and the Bowl by a grain each and took it with a Wheat set. From the wheat she sold Reina the Vase and the Deed. In Year 8 she paid 111 for the last Wooden Bowl, one over Reina, and took Graham's Rice seat: \"I've written mine down too.\" She finished the richest player at the table, with 570, one province below the seat she drew.",
  "Paddy drew the Rice seat, read the I Ching for 5 a year, and kept every promise he made. He climbed to Silk in Year 4 with the Jar, paid 70 for a Brush and 120 for the Wooden Bowl, and took the throne in Year 6. As Emperor he never taxed above 30%, paid half of every province's land, finished the Grand Canal, raised all four links of the Great Wall in one year, and in Year 9 built the Marble Pagoda and ended the game: \"Thank you all for an awful lot of fair dealing.\"",
  "Graham drew the Wheat seat and was the first to climb, taking Silke's Silk seat in Year 3 with 190 grain and a grudge he had written down. Paddy took it from him a year later, and he sold the Bowl that crowned Paddy, then the Bowl that put Silke in his Rice seat. He ended where he began, in the wheat with the Reservoir and ten squares of desert: a hold.",
  "The deck was chosen with Manish so that the population doubled before it grew by half: land opened in Year 5 at x2, while every province still fed itself. The table bought 38 squares, more than the next population card would have needed, and the crown never fell into debt. The Wall went up in Year 8 and the Pagoda in Year 9, three years before the second doubling. Nobody at the table was ever in debt."])
